module v2.test.claim.local_binding_shadow

import v2.test.claim.local_binding_shadow_peer { shadow_pred, shadow_sentinel, unshadowed_sentinel }
import v2.std.integer { Int }
import v2.std.logic { Bool }
import v2.std.live_tree { LiveTreeDisposition, SubstrateInputsOnly }

data live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition = SubstrateInputsOnly

// THE SILENT FACE LEADS, BECAUSE IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS BELOW FLOOR RATHER THAN A NAMING
// NUISANCE. A call site that spells a name held by BOTH a local binding and a module-level free
// function reaches the FREE FUNCTION. Where the two disagree in arity that refuses loudly; where
// they AGREE it returns a plausible wrong value and no diagnostic at all. Reproduced twice
// independently on live corpus symbols before this fixture existed: a local bound to a named fn
// and called as `p` returned a ParagraphBlock from gunbc.plans.md_helpers (visible only because
// an unrelated cross-representation equality guard tripped on `== 42`), and silent-bear-842's
// fabric observer consumed the same displacement as a Bool predicate -- constantly true, a fold
// over three elements returning 3 where the direct fold counts 2, with nothing to see.
//
// THE LAW, ESTABLISHED ON A COMPLETE GRID AND NOT INFERRED ACROSS IT. Two lanes measured three
// binding forms against two bound values independently and agree in all six cells:
//
//     binding form   value = named top-level fn   value = lambda
//     let            reaches the free function    reaches the local
//     parameter      reaches the free function    reaches the local
//     pattern        reaches the free function    reaches the local
//
// So the BINDING FORM IS NOT THE AXIS. An earlier revision of this finding said it was, from
// three arms that varied the form while holding the value constant, and was retracted: three
// arms agreeing on the wrong axis is not a result. The value is the axis, and the lambda column
// is carried below as a GREEN CONTROL rather than dropped, because without it a reader concludes
// every local binding is unsafe -- which is exactly what the retracted law said.
//
// AND THE COLLISION IS DECIDED AT THE CALL SITE, NOT AT THE BINDING (silent-bear-842, confirmed
// here independently). The SAME named-fn value is wrong when called as `shadow_pred(0)` and
// CORRECT when handed to a parameter spelled `pred` and applied there. That is why renaming
// fixes it, and it narrows the law usefully: a local holding a named function is unsafe only at
// a call site spelling its own name.
//
// PER-PATH QUALIFIER, so a reader running this the other way does not call it flaky
// (silent-bear-842, measured): under ENTRY-SCOPED resolution the collision depends on the
// module's import closure, and under WHOLE-CORPUS floor preparation it is UNCONDITIONAL -- the
// floor folds every witness through one prepared subject, so a module that imports nothing still
// meets the name. This fixture IMPORTS its shadowing symbol deliberately, so it reds on both
// paths and needs no whole-corpus pool to reproduce.
//
// MEASUREMENT HYGIENE, and it is the reason the qualifier above exists at all
// (silent-bear-842, from a wrong result they corrected): IMPORTS ARE MODULE-SCOPE, so an arm that
// adds an import VOIDS EVERY EARLIER ARM'S MEASUREMENT IN THAT FILE. Five arms measured green
// before the shadowing import was added all failed once it was present -- the arms did not
// change, the module's name pool did. So an A/B across arms is only valid when the import block
// is identical at both readings, which is a fact about how this defect is MEASURED rather than
// about the defect.

fn strictly_positive(n: Int) -> Bool {
  n > 0
}

type PredCarrier {
  shadow_pred: fn(Int) -> Bool
}

fn shadow_carrier_named() -> PredCarrier {
  PredCarrier { shadow_pred: strictly_positive }
}

fn shadow_carrier_lambda() -> PredCarrier {
  PredCarrier { shadow_pred: fn(n) { n > 0 } }
}

fn let_named() -> Bool {
  let shadow_pred = strictly_positive
  shadow_pred(0)
}

fn param_named(shadow_pred: fn(Int) -> Bool) -> Bool {
  shadow_pred(0)
}

fn pattern_named() -> Bool {
  match shadow_carrier_named() {
    PredCarrier { shadow_pred: shadow_pred } => shadow_pred(0)
  }
}

fn let_lambda() -> Bool {
  let shadow_pred = fn(n) { n > 0 }
  shadow_pred(0)
}

fn pattern_lambda() -> Bool {
  match shadow_carrier_lambda() {
    PredCarrier { shadow_pred: shadow_pred } => shadow_pred(0)
  }
}

fn apply_as_pred(pred: fn(Int) -> Bool) -> Bool {
  pred(0)
}

fn let_uncollided_named() -> Bool {
  let uncollided_pred = strictly_positive
  uncollided_pred(0)
}

fn let_named_applied_under_another_name() -> Bool {
  let shadow_pred = strictly_positive
  apply_as_pred(pred: shadow_pred)
}

fn pattern_named_applied_under_another_name() -> Bool {
  match shadow_carrier_named() {
    PredCarrier { shadow_pred: shadow_pred } => apply_as_pred(pred: shadow_pred)
  }
}

// THE ONE ENROLLED ROW. Every conjunct asserts the DESIRED law -- the local binding is what the
// call reaches -- so this is RED today and GREEN the day it is repaired. The three conjuncts are
// one identity rather than three because they are one defect measured on one axis, and because
// each returns a Bool: nothing here throws, so this row is an OBSERVED semantic red and never a
// no-verdict. That distinction is the whole subject of the lane that authored it.
//
// A LOUD FACE EXISTS AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT AN ARM HERE. Against a shadowing symbol of a
// DIFFERENT arity the call refuses (`call contract mismatch calling 'render': missing required
// argument 'proto'`, measured against std.layout). A refusal is a thrown claim, which produces no
// verdict at all, so expressing that face as a witness row would enrol exactly the non-verdict
// debt this fixture's lane exists to reduce. It is recorded above instead.
test fn local_named_function_binding_is_what_the_call_reaches() -> Bool {
  !let_named()
    && !param_named(shadow_pred: strictly_positive)
    && !pattern_named()
}

// GREEN CONTROLS. They pass today and locate the boundary: the defect is the VALUE, not the
// binding form, and not the act of binding locally at all.
test fn lambda_binding_is_unaffected_control() -> Bool {
  !let_lambda()
    && !param_named(shadow_pred: fn(n) { n > 0 })
    && !pattern_lambda()
}

// GREEN CONTROL for the call-site half: the same named-fn value, applied through a parameter
// spelled differently, reaches the local. This is what makes the law narrow enough to imply its
// own workaround.
test fn named_binding_applied_under_another_name_is_unaffected_control() -> Bool {
  !let_named_applied_under_another_name()
    && !pattern_named_applied_under_another_name()
}

// GREEN CONTROL that makes the COLLISION the subject rather than named-function values generally:
// the same named function, bound to a local whose name NOTHING shadows, called at that local's own
// spelling -- the exact shape that reds above -- reaches the local. So the wrong arms differ from
// this one in one variable only, the existence of a same-named free function.
test fn uncollided_named_binding_is_unaffected_control() -> Bool {
  !let_uncollided_named()
}

// ===========================================================================================
// THE PERMANENT ARMS. Everything above measures the defect on a Bool, which was enough to
// establish the law and is NOT enough to keep it. A Bool has two inhabitants, so a displaced call
// and a correct call can agree by coincidence; these arms hold every axis equal except the VALUE
// RETURNED, so a regression names the producer instead of merely disagreeing with it.
//
// EVERY AXIS THE DISPATCH NAMED IS HELD EQUAL between the local and the module function it
// collides with: same arity, same parameter type, same result type, both total. The only
// difference is the sentinel, and the local's sentinel is REQUIRED -- an arm asserting 2 cannot
// be satisfied by the free function, which answers 900 for every input including this one.
//
// THE SHADOWING SYMBOL IS FIXTURE-OWNED, which is how the "unrelated global renamed or removed"
// arm is answered here. That arm exists to catch a repair that works by accident of what else is
// in scope; the construction that forecloses it is that nothing outside this fixture's own two
// files can supply, rename or delete `shadow_sentinel`, so there is no unrelated global whose
// motion could change these results. That is stronger than an A/B over one rename, which only
// samples the globals someone thought to move.

fn local_sentinel(n: Int) -> Int {
  n + 1
}

fn sentinel_carrier_named() -> SentinelCarrier {
  SentinelCarrier { shadow_sentinel: local_sentinel }
}

type SentinelCarrier {
  shadow_sentinel: fn(Int) -> Int
}

fn let_sentinel_positional() -> Int {
  let shadow_sentinel = local_sentinel
  shadow_sentinel(1)
}

fn param_sentinel(shadow_sentinel: fn(Int) -> Int) -> Int {
  shadow_sentinel(1)
}

fn pattern_sentinel() -> Int {
  match sentinel_carrier_named() {
    SentinelCarrier { shadow_sentinel: shadow_sentinel } => shadow_sentinel(1)
  }
}

fn no_local_binding_reaches_the_module_function() -> Int {
  unshadowed_sentinel(1)
}

fn no_local_binding_reaches_the_shadowed_module_function() -> Int {
  shadow_sentinel(1)
}

// PERMANENT ARM 1 -- the local callable wins at every binding form. POSITIONAL ONLY, and that is
// a ruled property of the language rather than a gap in this fixture: a named-argument call
// through a function VALUE is a blocking refusal (CallNamedArgOnFunctionValue), operator-required,
// with direct declaration calls keeping named arguments and function-value calls staying
// positional-only. The named spelling is therefore measured where it lives -- at COMPILE, in
// test.claim.local_binding_shadow_compile_witness, which pins that the SHAPE tier and this
// DISPATCH tier consume ONE binding standing: shape sees a function value and refuses the label,
// dispatch sees the same function value and calls the local. Before the repair those two tiers
// disagreed about the same binding, which is what let a labeled call be silently accepted by the
// free function while the shape check was already calling it a function value.
test fn local_callable_wins_over_the_same_named_module_function() -> Bool {
  let_sentinel_positional() == 2
    && param_sentinel(shadow_sentinel: local_sentinel) == 2
    && pattern_sentinel() == 2
}

// PERMANENT ARM 2 -- the opposite direction, and the one a repair that simply stopped consulting
// the free-function table would break. With NO local binding competing for the spelling, the
// module function is still what the call reaches: 700 from a name nothing shadows, and 900 from
// the very name the arm above displaces. The second conjunct is the load-bearing one -- it proves
// the free-function tier was narrowed rather than removed.
test fn a_module_function_is_still_callable_with_no_local_binding() -> Bool {
  no_local_binding_reaches_the_module_function() == 700
    && no_local_binding_reaches_the_shadowed_module_function() == 900
}

// PERMANENT ARM 3 -- the sentinel is REQUIRED, not merely observed. Stated as its own row because
// the two arms above would both pass against an interpreter that answered 2 and 900 for reasons
// unrelated to which function ran: this one asserts the free function's answer is ABSENT from the
// shadowed positions and the local's answer is ABSENT from the unshadowed one, so each result is
// evidence about a producer rather than a coincidence of arithmetic.
test fn each_sentinel_is_absent_from_the_other_positions() -> Bool {
  let_sentinel_positional() != 900
    && pattern_sentinel() != 900
    && no_local_binding_reaches_the_shadowed_module_function() != 2
}

// ===========================================================================================
// THE STILL-OPEN HALF, ENROLLED AS AN EXPECTED RED RATHER THAN LEFT IN PROSE. The repair above
// closed the case where the local binding holds something CALLABLE. Where the local holds a
// NON-CALLABLE value the call still falls through to the module free function and returns its
// answer: `let shadow_sentinel = 5` followed by `shadow_sentinel(1)` evaluates to 900, measured by
// execution. That is the identical below-floor class -- a plausible wrong value, no diagnostic --
// with the callable half repaired and this half untouched.
//
// AND ITS COMPILE-SIDE TWIN IS ALREADY GREEN, which is what makes this worth a row rather than a
// note. v2.test.claim.local_binding_shadow_compile measures that a labeled call through this exact
// binding refuses CallNamedArgOnFunctionValue -- the SHAPE tier names the callee a function value
// even when the bound value is an Int -- while DISPATCH walks past the binding to the peer. Two
// tiers, one binding, opposite answers. The repair made them agree for callable bindings; they
// still disagree here.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT REPAIRED IN THE SAME CHANGE, stated as a scope decision rather than a discovery
// order. Refusing on ANY lexical binding rather than a callable one widens the gate over every
// spelling in the corpus that shadows a builtin with a non-callable value, and that population is
// not measured. A widening whose blast radius is unmeasured is exactly the shape that swallows its
// own controls, so it is filed here -- one row, one class, an OBSERVED semantic red that returns a
// Bool and never throws -- and repaired against a measured population.
//
// NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a census of lexical bindings whose spelling collides with a builtin or
// module free function and whose bound value is not callable. The row retires when that population
// is enumerated and the gate is widened to refuse over it.

fn noncallable_local_shadow() -> Int {
  let shadow_sentinel = 5
  shadow_sentinel(1)
}

test fn a_noncallable_local_binding_does_not_fall_through_to_the_module_function() -> Bool {
  noncallable_local_shadow() != 900
}
